Toward a Theory of Planetary State Estimation

A Formal Foundation for the Planetary State Core (PSC)

Thesis draft — v0.3

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Abstract

We develop a formal framework for estimating the biophysical state of a planet from heterogeneous, partial observations. The central object is the Planetary State Core (PSC): a joint estimator of state and of the epistemic conditions under which that state is asserted.

We introduce a typed uncertainty algebra distinguishing four classes — semantic, observational, stochastic, and model-theoretic — maintained separately by the PSC, and prove an impossibility theorem for canonical scalar aggregation over this algebra under explicitly stated model-expansion axioms. We distinguish two compatibility operators on credal sets (existential and universal), and identify their correct closure operators as registered objects. We define a state record \mathcal S whose invariants are executable at the schema level. We prove a separation theorem for detection, regime classification, and criticality. We introduce registered selections as the dual of registered aggregations, and prove a non-canonicity result under G-equivariance on credal sets with no invariant element. We conclude with a federated architecture and a temporally causal backtesting protocol.

