Part I — Foundations

Chapter 1. Introduction

1.1 The problem

Let E denote the Earth system. At each time t and location r \in E, there exists a biophysical configuration X(t,r), taking values in a priori unspecified space \mathcal X. We do not observe X directly. We observe partial signals

Y(t,r) = \{Y_i(t_i,r_i)\}_{i \in \mathcal I}

produced by heterogeneous instruments, each with its own spatial support, temporal resolution, calibration, failure mode, and inference chain between the physical quantity of interest and the recorded signal.

We ask: At any (t,r), what is the best available estimation of X(t,r), with what uncertainty, and under what epistemic conditions?

1.2 What this thesis is not

It is not a system for archiving human knowledge. It is not a governance instrument. It is not a decision system. It is a formal framework for stating, in a typed and auditable manner, what is known and not known about the biophysical state of a planet.

1.3 Contributions

1. A typed uncertainty algebra (§3) with four classes maintained separately by the PSC.
2. An impossibility theorem for canonical aggregation under explicit model-expansion axioms (§4).
3. Two compatibility operators on credal sets, with their closure operators as registered objects (§8).
4. A formal state record \mathcal S (§5–7) with executable invariants.
5. A separation theorem for detection, regime, and criticality (§9).
6. A non-canonicity theorem for selection from credal sets under G-equivariance (§13).
7. A federated architecture (§10–11) and a causal backtesting protocol (§14).

1.4 Relation to prior work

We draw on data assimilation [Talagrand 1997; Evensen 2009], imprecise probability [Walley 1991; Zaffalon 2002], credal networks [Cozman 2000], dilation theory [Seidenfeld & Wasserman 1993; Herron, Seidenfeld & Wasserman 1994, 1997; Bradley & Steele 2014], copula theory [Nelsen 2006; Genest & Nešlehová 2007], Wasserstein–Fisher–Rao geometry [Gallouët & Monsaingeon 2017], and the epistemology of measurement [Cartwright 1999; Chang 2004].

