Chapter 2. Mathematical Preliminaries

2.1 Measurable structure

Let (\mathcal X, \Sigma_{\mathcal X}) be a measurable space. \mathcal P(\mathcal X) denotes probability measures; \mathrm{Kern}(\mathcal X, \mathcal Y) denotes Markov kernels.

2.2 Credal sets

Definition 2.1. A credal set on \mathcal X is a closed convex set \mathcal C \subseteq \mathcal P(\mathcal X). \mathrm{Cred}(\mathcal X) denotes the collection of credal sets in the weak-* topology.

2.3 Lower and upper previsions

Definition 2.2. For a bounded real-valued function f: \mathcal X \to \mathbb R,

\underline P(f) = \inf_{P \in \mathcal C} \mathbb E_P[f], \qquad \overline P(f) = \sup_{P \in \mathcal C} \mathbb E_P[f].

Coherent lower previsions and credal sets are in one-to-one correspondence when the lower prevision is defined on the entire relevant gamble space. Restriction to a finite basis loses information (§8.7).

2.4 Spatio-temporal fields

We model (t,r) \in \mathcal T \times \mathcal R. A state field is a measurable map X: \mathcal T \times \mathcal R \to \mathcal X. A state record at resolution \rho is a discretization into subsets R_j with \mathrm{diam}(R_j) \leq \rho, together with an estimation of X on each.

