Chapter 4. Impossibility of Canonical Aggregation

(Corrected from v0.2: the proof uses a model-expansion axiom that is now stated explicitly.)

Definition 4.1. A scalar aggregation is a map \Gamma: \mathfrak U \to [0, \infty] where \mathfrak U is the space of typed uncertainties, and [0, \infty] carries the standard order topology.

We require:

(P1) Marginal consistency. If \mathcal U_{\mathrm{var}}, \mathcal U_{\mathrm{stoch}}, \mathcal U_{\mathrm{model}} are fixed and \mathcal U_{\mathrm{obs}} \preceq \mathcal U_{\mathrm{obs}}' in the Blackwell ordering, then \Gamma(\mathcal U) \leq \Gamma(\mathcal U').

(P2) Invariance under reparameterization. If F_\theta \mapsto F_{\phi(\theta)} is a bijection of \Theta_F preserving \mathfrak F, then \Gamma is unchanged.

(P3) Determinism compatible. If \mathcal U_{\mathrm{stoch}} is a Dirac kernel and \mathcal U_{\mathrm{model}} is a singleton, \Gamma depends only on \mathcal U_{\mathrm{obs}}.

(P4) Continuity and boundedness on compact families. Let \mathfrak K be a compact class of models, and let K(\mathfrak K) = \{\mathfrak F \subseteq \mathfrak K\} be the space of subfamilies of \mathfrak K in the Hausdorff metric. Then \Gamma \restriction K(\mathfrak K) is continuous.

(P5) Model expansion sensitivity. Let \mathfrak F_1 \subset \mathfrak F_2 \subseteq \mathfrak K. If there exists F \in \mathfrak F_2 \setminus \mathfrak F_1 and an observation history Y such that \mathcal C_F(Y) \not\subseteq \overline{\mathrm{conv}}\left(\bigcup_{F' \in \mathfrak F_1} \mathcal C_{F'}(Y)\right), then \Gamma(\mathfrak F_1 \text{ with fixed other components}) < \Gamma(\mathfrak F_2 \text{ with fixed other components}).

Theorem 4.2. There is no scalar aggregation \Gamma satisfying (P1)–(P5).

Proof. Fix \mathcal U_{\mathrm{var}}, \mathcal U_{\mathrm{obs}}, \mathcal U_{\mathrm{stoch}}, and a compact class \mathfrak K. Let \mathfrak F_0 = \{F_0\} \subset \mathfrak K. By (P5) applied to \mathfrak F_1 = \mathfrak F_0 and \mathfrak F_2 = \mathfrak F_0 \cup \{F_2\} where F_2 is chosen so that the hypothesis of (P5) is satisfied with some Y, we get \Gamma(\mathfrak F_0) < \Gamma(\mathfrak F_0 \cup \{F_2\}).

Iterate: construct \mathfrak F_n = \mathfrak F_0 \cup \{F_2, \ldots, F_n\} with n distinct models, each added satisfying the hypothesis of (P5) relative to the previous family. Then \Gamma(\mathfrak F_n) \geq \Gamma(\mathfrak F_0) + n \cdot \delta for some \delta > 0, hence \Gamma(\mathfrak F_n) \to \infty.

But K(\mathfrak K) is compact (in the Hausdorff metric for a compact \mathfrak K), and \Gamma continuous on it, so \Gamma is bounded on K(\mathfrak K). Contradiction. \square

Corollary 4.3. No scalar V can be computed from \mathcal U without additional assumptions not contained in \mathcal U.

Definition 4.4. An aggregation \Gamma is permitted if it is registered with declared domain of validity, assumptions, calibration procedure, and falsification tests.

Remark 4.5. The proof does not depend on \mathfrak K being a specific class of physical models. It uses only compactness and the existence of an expansion satisfying (P5). Where (P5) is contested — e.g., because added models are judged as "essentially redundant" — the theorem does not apply, and aggregation may be permitted. This is as it should be: the impossibility is conditional on the axiom, and the axiom itself is a claim open to dispute.

