Part IV — Estimation, Detection, Criticality

Chapter 8. Estimation Under Model Uncertainty

8.1 The estimator

Definition 8.1. A PSC estimator is a map

\mathcal E: \mathcal O^* \to \mathrm{Cred}(\mathcal X) \times \mathcal G

sending an observation history to a credal set and a coverage object.

8.2 The two compatibility operators

(Corrected from v0.2: the intersection of the previous section was a quantifier error.)

Definition 8.2. Let \mathfrak F be a non-empty class of admissible models, Y an observation history, and \mathcal C_F(Y) the posterior credal set under model F. Define:

\boxed{
\mathcal C_\exists(Y) := \bigcup_{F \in \mathfrak F} \mathcal C_F(Y)
\qquad \text{(existential compatibility)}
}

\boxed{
\mathcal C_\forall(Y) := \bigcap_{F \in \mathfrak F} \mathcal C_F(Y)
\qquad \text{(universal compatibility)}
}

The existential operator captures compatibility with at least one admissible model. The universal operator captures compatibility with all admissible models.

Proposition 8.3.

(i) \mathcal C_\forall(Y) \subseteq \mathcal C_\exists(Y).

(ii) If |\mathfrak F| = 1, then \mathcal C_\exists = \mathcal C_\forall.

(iii) \mathcal C_\exists(Y) need not be convex, even if every \mathcal C_F(Y) is.

(iv) Both operators are monotone under observation removal: if Y' \subseteq Y, then \mathcal C_\exists(Y) \subseteq \mathcal C_\exists(Y') and \mathcal C_\forall(Y) \subseteq \mathcal C_\forall(Y').

Proof. (i) If P is in every \mathcal C_F, it is in at least one. (ii) Trivial. (iii) Example: \mathfrak F = \{F_1, F_2\} with \mathcal C_{F_1} = \{P_1\}, \mathcal C_{F_2} = \{P_2\}, P_1 \neq P_2. Then \mathcal C_\exists = \{P_1, P_2\}, not convex. (iv) Removing observations removes constraints: \mathcal C_F(Y) \subseteq \mathcal C_F(Y') for each F; both union and intersection preserve this inclusion. \square

Remark 8.4. The two operators answer different questions. \mathcal C_\exists is the correct object for the question "what states could the world be in, given that at least one of our models is right?" \mathcal C_\forall is the correct object for the question "what states are compatible with every model we admit?" These are not the same, and conflating them — as the earlier draft did — is exactly the kind of error the PSC is supposed to prevent.

Proposition 8.5 (Recovering convexity). A credal set can be obtained from \mathcal C_\exists by applying a registered closure operator \kappa^{(k)}, such as the closed convex hull in the weak-* topology:

\kappa^{(k)}_{\mathrm{cvx}}(\mathcal C_\exists) = \overline{\mathrm{conv}}(\mathcal C_\exists).

By Invariant I13, this operator is registered, and any credal set produced from a model class carries a reference to the closure used.

Remark 8.6. The closure operation is itself an epistemic choice. Different closure operators (closed convex hull, \varepsilon-contamination, topological closure in a different topology) yield different credal sets. This is not a defect; it is a decision that must be recorded.

8.3 Envelope theorem (for the universal operator)

Theorem 8.7. Let \mathfrak F \neq \emptyset. Then

\mathcal C_\forall(Y) = \bigcap_{F \in \mathfrak F} \mathcal C_F(Y)

is a credal set (closed convex), and is the largest credal set compatible with every admissible model.

Proof. Each \mathcal C_F(Y) is a credal set. The intersection of closed convex sets is closed convex. Every element is in each \mathcal C_F(Y), hence compatible with every F \in \mathfrak F; any larger set would contain a P outside some \mathcal C_F(Y), hence incompatible with F. \square

8.4 Monotonicity under observation removal

Proposition 8.8. Let Y' \subseteq Y. Then for each k, \pi_k \mathcal C_\forall(Y) \subseteq \pi_k \mathcal C_\forall(Y').

Proof. Direct from Proposition 8.3(iv) and projection monotonicity. \square

Remark 8.9. This is Invariant I10, now stated for both operators. The universal operator is monotone; the existential operator is monotone as a set, but after closure it may not be, depending on the closure operator chosen. The registration of the closure (I13) makes this explicit.

8.5 Spatial dependency algebra (restricted)

Definition 8.10 (Admissible dependency). Let \{\mathcal C(r)\}_{r \in \mathcal R} be marginals. A dependency structure \Delta_{\mathrm{dep}} is:

(a) for continuous marginals in dimension d = 2: a set of admissible copulas \mathfrak C_\Delta \subseteq \mathcal P(\mathcal X^{\mathcal R}); or

(b) otherwise: an alternative registered dependency representation such as a graphical model, a vine copula, a constrained copula class, an optimal transport coupling, a set of moment constraints, or another explicitly declared structure.

Proposition 8.11 (Dimension restriction). In d = 2, the Fréchet–Hoeffding bounds give a canonical interval of copulas; the lower bound W(u_1, u_2) = \max\{u_1 + u_2 - 1, 0\} is a genuine copula representing countermonotonicity.

For d \geq 3, the lower Fréchet–Hoeffding bound is not a copula. There is no unique multivariate analogue of countermonotonicity obtained by replacing the bivariate lower bound by a d-dimensional copula. Alternative constructions (paired countermonotonicity, joint mixability, \Sigma-countermonotonicity) each define a class of couplings rather than a unique structure.

Proposition 8.12 (Discrete margins). If any marginal is discrete or mixed, the copula is not unique; the class of admissible copulas is infinite, and members can embody completely different dependence structures. The dependency algebra for discrete or mixed marginals must use an alternative registered representation, not a copula.

Corollary 8.13. Copula representation is insufficient or non-unique under these conditions. This does not imply that a graphical model is the only alternative; it implies that an explicit, registered dependency representation is required, and the choice is a modelling decision recorded in \Delta_{\mathrm{dep}}.

8.6 Credal updating and dilation

Definition 8.14 (Generalized Bayes rule). Given prior \mathcal C_t and observation Y_{t+1},

\mathcal C_{t+1}(Y_{t+1}) = \left\{ \frac{P(Y_{t+1} \mid X) P(X)}{\int P(Y_{t+1} \mid X') P(X') dX'} : P \in \mathcal C_t \right\}.

Theorem 8.15 (Dilation is possible and well-established). Dilation — the phenomenon in which the posterior marginal credal set over a coordinate is strictly wider than the prior, even under an informative observation — is an established possible phenomenon in credal inference. Seidenfeld and Wasserman (1993) established that under generalized Bayesian updating, dilation occurs if and only if the set of probabilities has non-empty intersection with the independence plane between the event and the observation. Herron, Seidenfeld, and Wasserman (1994, 1997) showed that dilation manifests asymptotically in certain robust Bayesian models. Bradley and Steele (2014) confirm that dilation remains a live problem for imprecise probabilism.

The hypotheses of bounded likelihood and compact model class do not exclude dilation.

Corollary 8.16. The mapping \mathcal C_t \mapsto \mathcal C_{t+1}(Y) is not a contraction in the Hausdorff or Wasserstein metric on credal sets. Martingale arguments apply to single measures, not to set-valued updates. The diameter of \mathcal C_{t+1} is not a martingale.

Corollary 8.17. Dilation must be recorded. Invariant I11 requires that any widening of the marginal credal set upon observation — measured by the marginal diameter \rho_k — be reported with the affected coordinate and observation. This is not a bug to be fixed; it is information about the limits of inference.

Remark 8.18. We do not claim that dilation is "generic" in a topological or measure-theoretic sense. The literature establishes its possibility and its asymptotic persistence in specific robust Bayesian settings; a genericity statement would require specifying a topology or measure on the space of credal states, which is not fixed by the framework. The operational requirement is that detected dilation be reported.

8.7 Previsions and lossiness

Definition 8.19 (Prevision basis). Let \{f_k\}_{k \in K} be a finite basis of gambles. The PSC stores (\underline P(f_k), \overline P(f_k))_{k \in K}.

Proposition 8.20 (Lossiness). The map \mathcal C \mapsto \{\underline P(f_k), \overline P(f_k)\}_k is a projection with nontrivial kernel. Distinct credal sets share the same prevision bounds on a finite basis.

Corollary 8.21. The record must carry prevision_basis_id and an explicit statement that the stored uncertainty is an envelope on a declared basis. Queries outside the basis cannot be answered without recomputation.

