Chapter 9. Separation of Detection, Regime, and Criticality

Theorem 9.1. There exist referentials \Theta_R^{(1)} \neq \Theta_R^{(2)} and a record \mathcal S such that D(\mathcal S) is identical under both, R^{(1)}(\mathcal S) \neq R^{(2)}(\mathcal S), and there exist \Theta_C^{(1)}, \Theta_C^{(2)} with C^{(1)}(\mathcal S) \neq C^{(2)}(\mathcal S).

Proof. Let \mathcal S record a -2\sigma anomaly in forest cover at rate -0.3\%/\mathrm{yr} over 10 years. Under \Theta_R^{(1)}, "decline" requires persistence over 10 years; under \Theta_R^{(2)}, over 20 years. Detection depends only on X and \Delta X, identical in both cases. Regime diverges; criticality follows. \square

Corollary 9.2. There is no referential-free regime or criticality classification.

