Part VI — Derivatives and Selections

Chapter 12. Derivative Semantics for Non-Scalar States

12.1 The transport derivative

Definition 12.1. Let X_t \in \mathcal P_2(\mathcal X) with \mathcal X Riemannian. Under transport semantics, the temporal derivative is the minimal-norm vector field v_t \in L^2(X_t) satisfying the continuity equation

\partial_t X_t + \nabla \cdot (X_t v_t) = 0,

with Wasserstein velocity \|\partial_t X\|_{\mathcal W_2} = (\int \|v_t\|^2 dX_t)^{1/2}.

12.2 The information derivative

Definition 12.2. Under information semantics, \partial_t X_t := \partial_t \log X_t(x), with Fisher–Rao speed

\|\partial_t X\|_{\mathcal F} = \left(\int (\partial_t \log X_t)^2 X_t dx\right)^{1/2}.

The Wasserstein–Fisher–Rao metric combines both geometries; the JKO splitting scheme performs a Wasserstein step then a Fisher–Rao step, with convergence established for reaction–advection–diffusion equations under compactness assumptions.

Proposition 12.3. The choice of geometry is a modelling decision, not a physical property of X. A distribution over temperature admits both transport semantics (where heat moves) and information semantics (how belief updates). Both are legitimate; they answer different questions and give non-equivalent \Delta X.

12.3 The credal derivative

Definition 12.4 (Epistemic diameter). For a bounded convex credal set \mathcal C,

\rho(\mathcal C) := \sup_{P, Q \in \mathcal C} W_2(P, Q).

This is well-defined for any bounded convex set, with no smoothness, nesting, or interior assumption.

Definition 12.5 (Epistemic derivative).

\partial_t^{\mathrm{epis}} \mathcal C_t := \frac{d}{dt} \rho(\mathcal C_t),

positive for dilation, negative for contraction.

Definition 12.6 (Ontological derivative). Given a selection rule \sigma: \mathcal C_t \to \mathcal P(\mathcal X),

\partial_t^{\mathrm{onto}} \mathcal C_t := \frac{d}{dt} \sigma(\mathcal C_t).

Proposition 12.7 (Independence of ontological and epistemic derivatives). There exist credal trajectories realizing:

(a) \partial_t^{\mathrm{epis}} \mathcal C_t = 0 with \partial_t^{\mathrm{onto}} \mathcal C_t \neq 0; and

(b) \partial_t^{\mathrm{onto}} \mathcal C_t = 0 with \partial_t^{\mathrm{epis}} \mathcal C_t > 0.

Construction (a). Let \mathcal C_t = \{\mu : \text{barycenter of } \mu = t, \ \mathrm{Var}(\mu) \leq 1\}. Let \sigma be the Wasserstein barycenter. Then \rho(\mathcal C_t) = 2 (constant), and \sigma(\mathcal C_t) = \delta_t, so \partial_t^{\mathrm{onto}} \mathcal C_t = \delta'_t \neq 0.

Construction (b). Let \mathcal C_t = \{\mu : \mu \text{ symmetric about } 0, \ \mathrm{Var}(\mu) \leq t\}. Let \sigma be the mean. Then \sigma(\mathcal C_t) = \delta_0 (constant), and \rho(\mathcal C_t) = 2\sqrt{t}, so \partial_t^{\mathrm{epis}} \mathcal C_t = 1/\sqrt{t} > 0.

Remark 12.8. The two derivatives are independent functionals on the space of credal trajectories: neither implies the other. The proof is by explicit construction, not by general argument.

---

Chapter 13. Registered Selections

(Corrected from v0.2: the involution proof was invalid. The following reformulation uses G-invariance directly.)

The PSC consistently needs to output something — a value, a derivative, a rate — from a credal set. But credal sets have no canonical element. The choice of a selection rule \sigma is a modelling decision of the same species as aggregation \Gamma and referential \Theta.

Definition 13.1. A selection from a credal set \mathcal C is a map \sigma: \mathrm{Cred}(\mathcal X) \to \mathcal P(\mathcal X) with \sigma(\mathcal C) \in \mathcal C.

Definition 13.2. A registered selection is a selection \sigma^{(k)} recorded in the selection registry with declared domain, assumptions, loss function, and falsification tests.

Theorem 13.3 (Non-canonicity under G-equivariance). Let G be a transformation group acting on \mathcal X. Suppose there exists a G-invariant credal set \mathcal C with no G-invariant element, i.e., \mathcal C^G := \{P \in \mathcal C : g \cdot P = P \ \forall g \in G\} = \emptyset. Then no G-equivariant selection \sigma exists on \mathcal C.

Proof. Suppose \sigma is G-equivariant and \mathcal C is G-invariant. For every g \in G, since g \cdot \mathcal C = \mathcal C, equivariance gives

g \cdot \sigma(\mathcal C) = \sigma(g \cdot \mathcal C) = \sigma(\mathcal C).

Hence \sigma(\mathcal C) \in \mathcal C^G. Contradiction with \mathcal C^G = \emptyset. \square

Example 13.4. Let \mathcal X = \mathbb R, G the group of translations x \mapsto x + b, and

\mathcal C = \{\mu : \mathrm{Var}(\mu) \leq 1\}.

Then \mathcal C is convex (variance is convex in \mu) and closed (variance is lower-semicontinuous). Under translation \mu \mapsto \mu_b, \mathrm{Var}(\mu_b) = \mathrm{Var}(\mu), so \mathcal C is G-invariant. A translation-invariant probability measure on \mathbb R must be the Lebesgue measure (up to normalization), which has infinite variance, so no element of \mathcal C is G-invariant. Therefore no translation-equivariant selection exists on \mathcal C.

Corollary 13.5. Every PSC output of a point estimate, a point derivative, or a point rate from a credal set references a registered selection \sigma^{(k)}.

Remark 13.6. The theorem establishes non-canonicity under a specified equivariance requirement, not non-existence of selections in general. Continuous selections from credal sets can exist under suitable topological hypotheses (Michael-type selection theorems, barycenter constructions under appropriate conditions). What is shown is that no selection is simultaneously equivariant under a group with the invariant-set property of Example 13.4 and compatible with the natural interiority condition. This is enough to make selection a registered operation.

Remark 13.7. This is the dual of Theorem 4.2 (no canonical aggregation). The framework now has two impossibility results: one for collapsing typed uncertainty to a scalar, one for collapsing a credal set to a point under symmetry. Both are recorded as registry requirements.

