Part VII — Evaluation and Limits

Chapter 14. Backtesting and Falsifiability

14.1 The protocol

Definition 14.1 (Temporally causal evaluation). Fix event E at T_E. An evaluation is temporally causal if for every t < T_E, the evaluation at t uses only Y_{t' \leq t}.

Definition 14.2 (Lead time). \tau_E = T_E - t_E^*, with t_E^* = \inf\{t : A(\mathcal S(t, r_E)) = 1\}.

Definition 14.3 (Detection utility). U_{\mathrm{det}} = f(\tau_E, \mathrm{FA}, \mathrm{SA}, \mathrm{Cal}).

14.2 The referential confound

Proposition 14.4. Backtesting on a fixed event set evaluates detector performance under a fixed \Theta_R. It does not evaluate \Theta_R itself.

Corollary 14.5. A backtest result cannot be reported as "the PSC is good at detecting degradation." It must be reported as "under \Theta_R^{(k)}, the PSC detects k/n events at median lead \tau with false alarm rate p."

14.3 Test families

Test A (Retrodiction). Given Y_{0:t}, reconstruct X_{t+k}.

Test B (Early detection). For events \{E_i\}, measure (\tau_i, \mathrm{FA}_i, \mathrm{SA}_i) under fixed \Theta_R^{(k)}.

Test C (Coverage robustness). Remove observations; verify that U and G respond per I10.

Test D (Blind-region). Mask r; require G to record extrapolation and X(r) to be typed \mathrm{undefined} or carry visible metadata.

Test E (Dilation recording). Construct an observation that is known to induce dilation; verify that the record reports \mathrm{dilation\_status} = \mathrm{present} with the affected coordinate.

