Appendix A. Mathematical Proofs and Detailed Derivations

A.1 Proof of Theorem 4.2 (Impossibility of Canonical Aggregation)

Theorem. There is no scalar aggregation \Gamma: \mathfrak U \to [0, \infty] satisfying (P1)–(P5).

Proof. We proceed in four steps.

Step 1 (Setup). Let \mathfrak K be a compact class of admissible models, and let K(\mathfrak K) = \{\mathfrak F \subseteq \mathfrak K\} denote the space of subfamilies of \mathfrak K equipped with the Hausdorff metric. By (P4), \Gamma \restriction K(\mathfrak K) is continuous, hence bounded on the compact set K(\mathfrak K). Write M := \sup_{\mathfrak F \in K(\mathfrak K)} \Gamma(\mathfrak F) < \infty.

Step 2 (Base case). Fix \mathcal U_{\mathrm{var}}, \mathcal U_{\mathrm{obs}}, \mathcal U_{\mathrm{stoch}}. Let \mathfrak F_0 = \{F_0\} \subset \mathfrak K. Choose F_2 \in \mathfrak K \setminus \{F_0\} and an observation history Y such that

\mathcal C_{F_2}(Y) \not\subseteq \overline{\mathrm{conv}}\left(\bigcup_{F' \in \mathfrak F_0} \mathcal C_{F'}(Y)\right).

Such a pair (F_2, Y) exists by hypothesis of (P5); the axiom is not vacuous.

Step 3 (Iteration). Construct inductively \mathfrak F_n = \mathfrak F_{n-1} \cup \{F_{n+1}\} where F_{n+1} satisfies the hypothesis of (P5) relative to \mathfrak F_{n-1} for some Y_n. By (P5),

\Gamma(\mathfrak F_n) \geq \Gamma(\mathfrak F_{n-1}) + \delta_n

for some \delta_n > 0. To obtain a uniform lower bound, we note that the hypothesis of (P5) is satisfied by every F \notin \mathfrak F_{n-1} whose posterior credal set under some Y is not contained in the closed convex hull of the union of posteriors of \mathfrak F_{n-1}. Since \mathfrak K is compact and the map F \mapsto \mathcal C_F(Y) is continuous (under the standard measurability assumptions on observation kernels), the set of such F is closed and non-empty. A standard compactness argument yields a uniform \delta > 0 across all n for which \mathfrak F_{n-1} \neq \mathfrak K. Hence

\Gamma(\mathfrak F_n) \geq \Gamma(\mathfrak F_0) + n\delta.

Step 4 (Contradiction). Take n > M/\delta. Then \Gamma(\mathfrak F_n) > M, contradicting the boundedness from Step 1. \square

Remark A.1. The proof is a compactness argument in disguise. It shows that (P5) forces \Gamma to grow linearly with the number of "essentially distinct" admissible models, which is impossible on a compact model class. The only way to avoid the contradiction is to deny (P5) — i.e., to claim that adding admissible models does not increase uncertainty. This is a substantial position, and it is precisely what the PSC framework is designed to make explicit and auditable.

---

A.2 Proof of Proposition 8.3 (Existential vs. Universal Compatibility)

Proposition. Let \mathfrak F \neq \emptyset. Then:

(i) \mathcal C_\forall(Y) \subseteq \mathcal C_\exists(Y).

(ii) If |\mathfrak F| = 1, \mathcal C_\exists = \mathcal C_\forall.

(iii) \mathcal C_\exists(Y) need not be convex.

(iv) Both operators are monotone under observation removal: if Y' \subseteq Y, then \mathcal C_\exists(Y) \subseteq \mathcal C_\exists(Y') and \mathcal C_\forall(Y) \subseteq \mathcal C_\forall(Y').

Proof.

(i) If P \in \mathcal C_F(Y) for every F \in \mathfrak F, then in particular P \in \mathcal C_{F_0}(Y) for at least one F_0.

(ii) If \mathfrak F = \{F_0\}, both operators reduce to \mathcal C_{F_0}(Y).

(iii) Let \mathfrak F = \{F_1, F_2\}, \mathcal C_{F_1}(Y) = \{P_1\}, \mathcal C_{F_2}(Y) = \{P_2\} with P_1 \neq P_2. Then \mathcal C_\exists = \{P_1, P_2\}, which is not convex.

(iv) Removing observations removes constraints: for each F, \mathcal C_F(Y) \subseteq \mathcal C_F(Y'). Union and intersection preserve inclusion: if A \subseteq A' and B \subseteq B', then A \cup B \subseteq A' \cup B' and A \cap B \subseteq A' \cap B'. \square

Remark A.2. Part (iii) is the reason Invariant I13 (registered closure) is necessary. The existential operator does not produce a credal set by itself; a closure operator must be applied and registered.

---

A.3 Proof of Theorem 8.7 (Envelope Theorem)

Theorem. Let \mathfrak F \neq \emptyset. Then

\mathcal C_\forall(Y) = \bigcap_{F \in \mathfrak F} \mathcal C_F(Y)

is a credal set (closed convex), and is the largest credal set compatible with every admissible model.

Proof.

Closed. The intersection of closed sets is closed.

Convex. The intersection of convex sets is convex.

Largest. Suppose \mathcal C' \supsetneq \mathcal C_\forall(Y). Then there exists P \in \mathcal C' \setminus \mathcal C_\forall(Y). By definition, P \notin \mathcal C_F(Y) for some F \in \mathfrak F, hence P is not compatible with F. Therefore \mathcal C' contains an element incompatible with an admissible model, so \mathcal C' is not "compatible with every admissible model." \square

---

A.4 Proof of Theorem 8.15 (Dilation is Possible and Well-Established)

Theorem. Dilation is an established possible phenomenon in credal inference. Under generalized Bayesian updating, dilation occurs if and only if the set of probabilities has non-empty intersection with the independence plane between the event and the observation.

Proof sketch. This is Theorem 4.1 of Seidenfeld and Wasserman (1993). The "if and only if" characterisation is established by showing that dilation occurs precisely when the set of probabilities contains a measure under which the event and the observation are independent. The direction (\Leftarrow) follows by exhibiting a measure in the credal set under which conditioning leaves the marginal invariant, while other measures in the set shift the marginal in opposite directions. The direction (\Rightarrow) follows by contradiction: if every measure in the set exhibits dependence in a uniform direction, the posterior credal set cannot strictly contain the prior. \square

Remark A.3. The result is exact, not asymptotic. Herron, Seidenfeld, and Wasserman (1994, 1997) showed that dilation can persist asymptotically in certain robust Bayesian models, and Bradley and Steele (2014) confirmed dilation remains a live problem for imprecise probabilism. The PSC does not claim genericity in a topological or measure-theoretic sense; it claims possibility and requires recording.

---

A.5 Proof of Theorem 13.3 (Non-Canonicity Under G-Equivariance)

Theorem. Let G be a transformation group acting on \mathcal X. Suppose there exists a G-invariant credal set \mathcal C with

\mathcal C^G := \{P \in \mathcal C : g \cdot P = P \ \forall g \in G\} = \emptyset.

Then no G-equivariant selection \sigma: \mathrm{Cred}(\mathcal X) \to \mathcal P(\mathcal X) exists on \mathcal C.

Proof. Suppose \sigma is G-equivariant and \mathcal C is G-invariant. For every g \in G, since g \cdot \mathcal C = \mathcal C,

g \cdot \sigma(\mathcal C) = \sigma(g \cdot \mathcal C) = \sigma(\mathcal C).

Hence \sigma(\mathcal C) \in \mathcal C^G. Contradiction. \square

Example A.4. Let \mathcal X = \mathbb R, G the group of translations x \mapsto x + b, and

\mathcal C = \{\mu : \mathrm{Var}(\mu) \leq 1\}.

Convexity. Variance is convex in \mu: for \lambda \in [0,1],

\mathrm{Var}(\lambda \mu_1 + (1-\lambda)\mu_2) \leq \lambda \mathrm{Var}(\mu_1) + (1-\lambda)\mathrm{Var}(\mu_2) \leq 1.

Closedness. Variance is lower-semicontinuous in the weak topology; the sublevel set is closed.

G-invariance. Under translation \mu \mapsto \mu_b, \mathrm{Var}(\mu_b) = \mathrm{Var}(\mu), so \mathcal C is G-invariant.

No invariant element. A translation-invariant probability measure on \mathbb R must be proportional to Lebesgue measure, which has infinite variance. Hence \mathcal C^G = \emptyset.

Therefore no translation-equivariant selection exists on \mathcal C.

Remark A.5. Michael-type selection theorems establish that continuous selections exist for lower-semicontinuous set-valued maps with closed convex values into Banach spaces under paracompactness conditions. The present theorem does not contradict these results: it shows that no selection is simultaneously G-equivariant and compatible with the natural invariance requirement. The non-existence is conditional on equivariance, not on continuity alone.

---

A.6 Construction for Proposition 12.7 (Independence of Ontological and Epistemic Derivatives)

Proposition. There exist credal trajectories realizing:

(a) \partial_t^{\mathrm{epis}} \mathcal C_t = 0 with \partial_t^{\mathrm{onto}} \mathcal C_t \neq 0;

(b) \partial_t^{\mathrm{onto}} \mathcal C_t = 0 with \partial_t^{\mathrm{epis}} \mathcal C_t > 0.

Construction (a). Let

\mathcal C_t = \{\mu \in \mathcal P_2(\mathbb R) : \text{mean}(\mu) = t, \ \mathrm{Var}(\mu) \leq 1\}.

The Wasserstein diameter is

\rho(\mathcal C_t) = \sup_{\mu, \nu \in \mathcal C_t} W_2(\mu, \nu).

For any \mu, \nu with means t and variances \leq 1, the Wasserstein-2 distance is bounded by 2 (attained by two Dirac masses at t \pm 1). Hence \rho(\mathcal C_t) = 2 for all t, so \partial_t^{\mathrm{epis}} \mathcal C_t = 0.

Let \sigma be the Wasserstein barycenter. Then \sigma(\mathcal C_t) = \delta_t, so

\partial_t^{\mathrm{onto}} \mathcal C_t = \frac{d}{dt} \delta_t = \delta'_t \neq 0.

Construction (b). Let

\mathcal C_t = \{\mu \in \mathcal P_2(\mathbb R) : \mu \text{ symmetric about } 0, \ \mathrm{Var}(\mu) \leq t\}.

The Wasserstein diameter is \rho(\mathcal C_t) = 2\sqrt{t}, so

\partial_t^{\mathrm{epis}} \mathcal C_t = \frac{1}{\sqrt{t}} > 0.

Let \sigma be the mean. Then \sigma(\mathcal C_t) = \delta_0 for all t, so

\partial_t^{\mathrm{onto}} \mathcal C_t = 0.

\square

