Appendix E. Annotated Bibliography

E.1 Dilation and Imprecise Probability

Seidenfeld, T., & Wasserman, L. (1993). "Dilation for sets of probabilities." Annals of Statistics, 21(3), 1139–1154.

The foundational paper establishing dilation: conditioning on an event can make upper and lower probabilities less precise, even when the event is informative. Provides the characterisation used in Theorem 8.15.

Herron, T., Seidenfeld, T., & Wasserman, L. (1994). "The extent of dilation of sets of probabilities and the asymptotics of robust Bayesian inference." PSA: Proceedings of the Biennial Meeting of the Philosophy of Science Association, 1994(1), 250–259.

Studies the asymptotic behaviour of dilation in robust Bayesian models. Establishes that dilation does not vanish in general as evidence accumulates.

Herron, T., Seidenfeld, T., & Wasserman, L. (1997). "Divisive conditioning: Further results on dilation." Philosophy of Science, 64(3), 411–444.

Extends the 1993 and 1994 results, characterises dilation-prone sets of probabilities, and explores the connection with nonconglomerability.

Bradley, S., & Steele, K. (2014). "Should we avoid dilation?" Proceedings of the 14th International Conference on Principles of Knowledge Representation and Reasoning.

Surveys strategies for avoiding or mitigating dilation and argues that dilation remains a live problem for imprecise probabilism.

E.2 Copula Theory

Nelsen, R. B. (2006). An Introduction to Copulas (2nd ed.). Springer.

Standard reference for copula theory, including Sklar's theorem, Fréchet–Hoeffding bounds, and the dimension restriction (no minimum copula for d \geq 3).

Genest, C., & Nešlehová, J. (2007). "A primer on copulas for count data." ASTIN Bulletin, 37(2), 475–515.

Definitive treatment of copulas for discrete marginals. Shows that the copula is not unique when any marginal is discrete, and that familiar continuous-case relations are invalidated by ties. The primary source for Proposition 8.12.

Lee, W., Cheung, K. C., & Ahn, J. Y. (2016). "Multivariate countermonotonicity and the minimal copulas." Journal of Statistical Planning and Inference, 178, 1–12.

Establishes that the Fréchet–Hoeffding lower bound is not a copula for d \geq 3 and surveys alternatives (paired countermonotonicity, joint mixability, \Sigma-countermonotonicity). The source for Proposition 8.11.

E.3 Imprecise Probability and Credal Sets

Walley, P. (1991). Statistical Reasoning with Imprecise Probabilities. Chapman and Hall.

Foundational monograph on coherent lower previsions and their equivalence with credal sets. The source for Definition 2.2 and the prevision–credal correspondence.

Zaffalon, M. (2002). "The naive credal classifier." Journal of Statistical Planning and Inference, 105(1), 5–21.

Introduces credal classifiers and demonstrates the computational tractability of credal inference in specific structured settings.

Cozman, F. G. (2000). "Credal networks." Artificial Intelligence, 120(2), 199–233.

Extends Bayesian networks to credal sets. Foundational for the federated estimator architecture.

Miranda, E., & Zaffalon, M. (2007). "Coherent lower previsions in systems modelling: products and aggregation rules." Reliability Engineering & System Safety, 92(6), 724–733.

Studies the geometry and continuity of the credal set operator. Relevant to Problem 15.10 (closure operator algebra).

E.4 Optimal Transport and Wasserstein–Fisher–Rao Geometry

Gallouët, T. O., & Monsaingeon, L. (2017). "A JKO splitting scheme for Kantorovich–Fisher–Rao gradient flows." SIAM Journal on Mathematical Analysis, 49(2), 1100–1130.

Establishes convergence of the JKO splitting scheme for the Kantorovich–Fisher–Rao metric, with a Wasserstein step followed by a Fisher–Rao step. The source for the convergence claim in §12.2.

Chizat, L., Peyré, G., Schmitzer, B., & Vialard, F.-X. (2018). "An interpolating distance between optimal transport and Fisher–Rao metrics." Foundations of Computational Mathematics, 18(1), 1–44.

Introduces the Wasserstein–Fisher–Rao metric independently and studies its geometry.

Peyré, G., & Cuturi, M. (2019). Computational Optimal Transport. Now Publishers.

Standard reference for computational aspects of Wasserstein distances, including the Sinkhorn algorithm and its applications to barycenter computation.

E.5 Selection Theory

Michael, E. (1956). "Continuous selections I." Annals of Mathematics, 63(2), 361–382.

Foundational paper on continuous selections from set-valued maps with closed convex values into Banach spaces under paracompactness conditions. The source for the Michael-type existence results referenced in Remark A.5.

Castellani, M., & Giuli, M. (2023). "A modified Michael's selection theorem with application to generalized Nash equilibrium problem." Journal of Optimization Theory and Applications, 196, 199–211.

Recent extension of Michael's theorem to non-metrizable range spaces and set-valued maps without closed values. Relevant to the topological hypotheses under which selections exist.

E.6 Data Assimilation

Talagrand, O. (1997). "Assimilation of observations, an introduction." Journal of the Meteorological Society of Japan, 75(1B), 191–209.

Standard introduction to data assimilation in geophysics, covering variational and sequential methods.

Evensen, G. (2009). Data Assimilation: The Ensemble Kalman Filter (2nd ed.). Springer.

Comprehensive treatment of ensemble Kalman filtering, the dominant approach in operational geophysical estimation.

E.7 Epistemology of Measurement

Cartwright, N. (1999). The Dappled World: A Study of the Boundaries of Science. Cambridge University Press.

Argues against the universality of physical laws and for the locality of scientific modelling. Relevant to the model uncertainty framework.

Chang, H. (2004). Inventing Temperature: Measurement and Scientific Progress. Oxford University Press.

Historical and philosophical study of how measurement standards are established and revised. Relevant to the semantic uncertainty framework and the versioning of variable definitions.

