Appendix F. Implementation Notes

F.1 Schema-Level Enforcement of Invariants

The invariants I1–I13 are executable at the schema level if the record is stored in a typed data structure with the following constraints:

1. No bare scalars for uncertainty. Any field of type uncertainty is a reference to an entry in an uncertainty registry, not a floating-point number. Fields of type float appearing in an uncertainty context are ill-typed.
2. No implicit aggregation. Any scalar derived from typed uncertainty must reference a registered \Gamma^{(k)}. The derivation graph is stored in the provenance block.
3. No unregistered closure. Any credal set derived from a model class must reference a registered \kappa^{(k)}.
4. No unregistered selection. Any point output from a credal set must reference a registered \sigma^{(k)}.
5. No referential-free regime or criticality. Fields R and C must carry a reference to a \Theta_R^{(k)} or \Theta_C^{(k)}, or be explicitly typed undefined.
6. No semantic incompatibility. \Delta X operations are typed only when the variable definitions are marked compatible.

F.2 Storage Architecture

The PSC record is stored as a directed acyclic graph of typed references:

· Observation nodes record raw instrument outputs and inference chains.
· Model nodes record admissible models and admissibility protocols.
· Credal nodes record credal sets (as prevision envelopes on a declared basis).
· Registry nodes record aggregations, closures, selections, and referentials.
· Record nodes link the above into a state record.

This structure permits efficient querying: "what observations constrained X_k at (t,r)?", "what closure operator produced this credal set?", "what referential was used for this criticality classification?"

F.3 Backtesting Implementation

The backtesting protocol (Chapter 14) is implemented as follows:

1. Temporal partition. Split the observation history at t. Observations with timestamp \leq t are visible; those with timestamp > t are hidden.
2. Evaluation. For each test family (A–E), run the estimator at t and record the output.
3. Metric computation. For Test B, compute lead time \tau_E = T_E - t_E^*, false alarm rate, spatial accuracy, and calibration. For Test C, verify that removing observations does not narrow the credal set on constrained variables. For Test E, construct a dilation-inducing observation and verify that dilation_status is set.
4. Referential freeze. \Theta_R^{(k)} is declared before the test and not modified during evaluation.

F.4 Open Implementation Questions

· Computational tractability. Credal inference at planetary resolution is intractable with current methods. Practical implementations will require approximations, and these must be registered as transformations.
· Storage of credal sets. Prevision envelopes on a finite basis lose information (Proposition 8.20). The basis must be chosen to cover the queries of interest, and this choice must be recorded.
· Propagation of dilation. When dilation occurs in a sub-domain, how does it propagate to the fused planetary state? This is an open problem (Problem 15.9).

